WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:09.620
What is it life?
What makes it different from just matter?

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:15.929
Everything living or not is just
chemicals made from atoms. These fish and

00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:21.180
the water of the fishery in are just
soups of atoms but what makes the fish

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:26.189
alive and not the water, is how the atoms
are organized, by a special kind of

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:29.009
molecule and you know this because you
learned about it in high school...

00:00:29.009 --> 00:00:34.500
DNA. The double helix molecule that
houses the chemical alphabet of A's and

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:42.530
C's and T's and G's. Which in different
combinations can make a flower or a frog

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:50.489
for you. Every living creature has its
code that makes it grow, reproduce and

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:58.109
change. DNA turns dust into life and how
it does that I will now here's the

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:00.500
mystery.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:07.300
20 years ago scientists learned to read
a creature's entire DNA sequence

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.070
beginning to end a genome it's called
they did this for simple things like

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:19.990
bacteria, and then for more complex flies,
and eventually for humans. And then they

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:27.640
began to tinker to take the glow from a
jellyfish and transfer it to account to

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.980
make critters do what they'd never done
before.

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:35.560
And as biologists got better at this as
Stanford professor Drew Endy, a new kind

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:42.360
of science was born: "called synthetic
biology it's a means to an end."

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:48.610
What can we do with the new tools of
synthetic biology? Well they can improve

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:53.530
on what's been spelled out over 3
billion years of evolution.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:02:00.310
We can take it beyond reading genomes or
editing genomes and start writing

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:06.580
genomes our own ideas of what life
should be like making creatures

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:10.750
drastically different from any that have
ever existed. And how do you do that? Well

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:15.090
already one group of scientists have
rewritten and rebuilt the entire

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:21.520
instruction kit for the very humble
little yeast. And they plan to put their

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.380
new recipe into a cell and if everything
goes according to plan it will come

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:32.020
alive and begin making baby yeast
exactly like it's most unusual parent it

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:37.919
will be new in the world.

00:02:38.879 --> 00:02:46.420
This synthetic yeast will break the
continuous chain of evolution that links

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:54.420
every creature back to the first living
cell it will be discontinuous in a way, a

00:02:54.420 --> 00:03:03.909
thing unto itself. And then where do we
take this new technology,? How far should

00:03:03.909 --> 00:03:09.269
we or will we go?

